{
  "headline": "UAE prosecutor says Flydubai co-pilot used crash axe in attempted terrorist attack",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "A United Arab Emirates prosecutor general stated on Saturday that the co-pilot of Flydubai flight FZ1073 used a crash axe to attempt a “terrorist attack” during an Israel-bound flight. Gen Hamad Saif al-Shamsi, the UAE prosecutor, told the state news agency WAM that investigations are ongoing to uncover the circumstances, motives, and related connections of the incident. The aircraft, which was carrying 182 people, plunged 16,625 feet in just over 30 seconds on September 30, 2026, before passengers and crew intervened to stop the co-pilot's attempt to take control. The extreme descent resulted in part of the plane's rudder being ripped off. Following the incident, two other pilots in the cabin took control and landed the aircraft safely in Tabuk, Saudi Arabia. The Flydubai captain, an Indian national identified as Smit Machchhar, sustained wounds to his head, hands, and neck. Machchhar reported to Indian Prime Minister Narendra Modi that he used his remaining energy to unlock the cockpit door, allowing others to enter, subdue the co-pilot, and stabilize the plane."
    },
    {
      "text": "Media outlets have identified the co-pilot as Hammam al-Hammami, an Omani national. Reports from the Wall Street Journal and the Associated Press indicate that al-Hammami had previously been barred from flying by Oman due to concerns regarding extremist or radical ideological views. Following that ban, he was reportedly moved to an administrative role at Oman Air before being hired by the UAE-based Flydubai. It remains unclear to what extent he was vetted during the Flydubai hiring process. While investigators had previously sought to determine how a weapon might have been smuggled onboard, Gen Hamad Saif al-Shamsi noted that the crash axe is an implement typically kept inside the cockpit for emergencies, such as fires or crashes. Security agencies from four regional countries, including Israel, are working to establish whether the suspect acted as a “lone wolf” or had connections to extremist groups, with assistance from the United States and other Western governments. On October 2, 2026, Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu stated the co-pilot had undergone “Islamist radicalization,” though he provided no evidence."
    }
  ]
}

