  "headline": "More than 1,200 Colorado students earn climate literacy seal",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "More than 1,200 students in Colorado have earned a climate literacy seal, a statewide initiative that combines climate-focused coursework with community service. Since Colorado lawmakers approved the seal in 2024, 26 of the state’s 179 school districts have adopted the program, according to the education non-profit Lyra Colorado. To earn the endorsement for their high school diplomas and transcripts, students must complete at least two advanced courses covering Earth’s climate systems and an approved project addressing a local climate issue. Anthony Muñoz, a student at Vista Academy, completed a capstone project by building an “ozone garden” using native plants to detect ground-level ozone pollution. For this project, Muñoz worked with climate scientists from the National Center for Atmospheric Research in Boulder to collect data on air quality and the impact of milkweed on monarch butterfly migration. The model for the seal is expanding to other states; Maine adopted a similar seal earlier this year, and California recently signed a bill to establish one for its graduates. Youth coalitions have also pursued the policy in Hawaii and Minnesota."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Toronto Fire Services launches Fire Prevention Week 2026 focusing on safe charging",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Toronto Fire Services is encouraging residents to prioritize fire safety during Fire Prevention Week 2026, which is scheduled to take place from Sunday, October 4 to October 3, 2026. The theme for this year is “Safe Charging Is a Superpower,” a campaign centered on charging habits intended to help prevent lithium-ion battery fires in homes and communities. Lithium-ion batteries are used in various daily devices, including smartphones, tablets, e-bikes, e-scooters, and other portable electronics. While generally safe when used properly, Toronto Fire Services notes that damaged, defective, or improperly charged batteries can pose a serious fire risk. Jim Jessop, Fire Chief of Toronto Fire Services, stated that safe charging involves using approved equipment and following manufacturer instructions, such as using approved chargers, cords, and batteries, avoiding tampering with or modifying devices, and replacing any damaged or defective charging equipment."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "BGMEA president urges Pakistan to increase imports from Bangladesh to address trade imbalance",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Mahmud Hasan Khan, President of the Bangladesh Garment Manufacturers and Exporters Association (BGMEA), has urged Pakistan to increase its imports of Bangladeshi products to address an existing trade imbalance. During a bilateral meeting in Uttara, Dhaka, Khan specifically identified high-quality jute yarn and life-saving pharmaceuticals as key sectors for increased exports to Pakistan. He noted that while Bangladesh imports significant volumes of essential raw materials, such as chemicals and dyes for the ready-made garment (RMG) sector, from Pakistan, Bangladeshi exports to Pakistan remain disproportionately low. The meeting included Pakistani High Commissioner to Bangladesh Imran Haidar and took place on a Sunday, though the specific calendar date was not established by the report from The Financial Express Bangladesh. To strengthen economic cooperation, Khan also called for the modernization of logistics to address excessive time required for direct maritime transportation."
    },
    {
      "text": "In response to the proposals, High Commissioner Imran Haidar expressed interest and assured BGMEA leadership that measures would be taken to boost two-way trade. Haidar suggested the exchange of high-level business delegations to explore market opportunities, stating that the High Commission would organize Business-to-Business (B2B) meetings and matchmaking sessions with Pakistani importers, brands, and trade bodies if a Bangladeshi delegation visits. Discussions also included potential market access strategies, such as formulating a bilateral special Free Trade Agreement (FTA) mechanism to sustain trade flows following Bangladesh's graduation from LDC status under SAFTA. Additionally, the parties discussed institutional development, including technology transfer and student exchange programs between the BGMEA University of Fashion & Technology (BUFT) and Pakistani textile institutes."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Iran says Strait of Hormuz will remain closed until seven conditions are met",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Iranian Parliament Speaker Mohammad Bagher Ghalibaf stated on Sunday that the Strait of Hormuz will not reopen until seven conditions, outlined in an interim understanding reached with the United States last June, are fulfilled. Ghalibaf, speaking via the state-linked news agency Nournews, said the decision follows Tehran receiving a U.S. response to its diplomatic proposal. He noted that Washington recently submitted new proposals through a mediator, despite what he described as differences between those proposals and public statements. Ghalibaf stated that Tehran would no longer accept stalling or the imposition of unilateral demands, asserting that the resumption of navigation through the strait is tied to the fulfillment of conditions based on the Islamabad memorandum of understanding. He added that Iran would both fight and negotiate and is prepared to confront the U.S. with new surprises on the military battlefield. Last week, U.S. President Donald Trump ruled out an Iranian proposal to reopen the strait, which is used to ship one-fifth of global oil and natural gas during peacetime."
    },
    {
      "text": "Iranian Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi told foreign ambassadors and diplomats in Tehran that Iran has clearly explained its conditions for reopening the waterway and hopes Washington will choose diplomacy. Araghchi stated that Iran is serious about finding diplomatic solutions but warned that if the U.S. takes military action again, Iran is more prepared than before and will take necessary action to defend itself. Additionally, Iranian Ministry of Foreign Affairs spokesman Esmaeil Baghaei noted that Tehran used the United Nations General Assembly platform last month to present its position regarding the end of the U.S.-Israel war on Iran."
    }
  ]
}
